  "final_text": "All four steps are done and verified against Kibana 9.5.5.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (`kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 26 fields. The time field is `timestamp`. The fields include `Carrier` (string), `Cancelled` (boolean), and `timestamp` (date), plus numeric, geo, and other string fields.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-98c360da Flights' (ID `80bbfec2-e6e6-4fbd-a69d-bbb8dbb59dda`) has exactly two panels, both on the data view by reference:\n   - \"Flights over time\": a line chart of count by date histogram on `timestamp`.\n   - \"Flights per carrier\" (later renamed in step 4): a donut of count split by `Carrier` (top 10).\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-98c360da Cancelled flights' (ID `44c3ff88-5363-492a-bc36-801b46c1af00`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_flights`, using `timestamp`. It counts documents matching `Cancelled: true` over a 5-minute window, runs every 1 minute, and fires when the count is greater than 10. It was created enabled, so no enabling was needed. Its last execution status is `ok`.\n\n4. **Rename:** The carrier panel is now titled \"Flights by carrier\". I read the dashboard back and it has two panels: \"Flights over time\" and \"Flights by carrier\". I read the rule back and it is enabled.\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change:\n- I used a donut chart for \"by carrier\" rather than a bar chart.\n- The rule has no actions (no connector or notification), so it only evaluates and records alerts.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
